      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Temgesic Injection (buprenorphine), a potent analgesic agent, was given to 240 patients under 18 years of age during a year of monitored release. All but four had the product for the management of moderate or severe pain in the immediate post-operative period. Analgesia was reported as adequate or good in 90% of these young patients when it was assessed 2 and 4 hours after infection. There were no reports of side-effects commonly associated with strong analgesics and particularly antagonist-analgesics such as confusion, hallucination, blurred vision, dry mouth and lightheadedness. There were no serious respiratory or cardiovascular effects. The incidences of other events did not differ from those recorded in the much larger adult population of almost 8,000 patients. Buprenorphine is an effective analgesic suitable for use in the young post-operative patient."},
